AQHow do I mock out a Slang function or Typed Structure?
ANSWER
EDIT
INFO
HISTORY
You want to test some Slang code in isolation from its dependency, which is a publicly scoped Slang function.

If you have a Java or .NET background you may be familiar with isolation frameworks which address similar concerns, such as EasyMock, Mockito or NMock.

For the remainder of this page we'll assume the target dependency is a function @My::Dependency( Double( x ) ) Returns( Double() ) ).

Function Stub / Spy
A stub replaces your dependency, providing controlled input to your code under test.

Example:


   Link( "_Slang RegTest Stub Function" );

   RegTestStubFunction( Structure(
           "_LIB Some Lib::My::Dependency", 8, 
   ) )
   {
       // In here, calling @My::Dependency( whatever ) always returns 8,
       // regardless of the arguments, if any, passed to My::Dependency
       @My::Function Under Test( );
   };
Note that if My::Dependency does not return anything - has a Returns() signature - and you want to make the function a no-op in the test, you'd use Null as its return value in the structure passed to RegTestStubFunction. A function with a Returns() signature actually returns Null.

RegTestStubFunction records some state too, so you can verify the stub was called.


   Assert( @RegTest Stub::Get Call Count( "My::Dependency" ) == 1 );
To make the stub more like a fake implementation of the dependency, pass a Lambda and it will be called instead of @My::Dependency in the block.
You can put Asserts in your Lambda to make it more like a mock.
Note that call count is not tracked if you are targeting a function that takes an argument passed by reference. (FIXED in IS:6012-178001)
Private Functions
Avoid stubbing out private functions, since by definition they are an implementation detail. But if necessary use Private as the scope:


   RegTestStubFunction( Structure(
           "_LIB Some Lib::Private::Fn Name", Value,    // private name
   ) )
   { ... }
Protected Functions
In order to stub out a protected function, you must use the scope of the Library Stub e.g.

   RegTestStubFunction( Structure( 
           "_LIB Stub Lib::Protected::Fn Name", Value
   ) )
   { ... }
Function Mock
A mock replaces your dependency, and provides an API for verifying the calls made.

Two ways to go:

Moxie::Function
Moxie::Function uses the easymock record/replay style:


 Link( "_TYPE Moxie" );
 // record
 Moxie = Moxie::Function( Target:= "My::Dependency" )
     .Call Any(). Returning( 7 ) // arguments not verified
     .Call( 0 ) . Returning( 6 ) // arguments verified
     .Call( 8 ) . Returning( 7 ) // arguments verified
 ;
 // replay
 RegTestStubFunction( Moxie.Overrides() )
 {
     @My::dependency( 2 ); // returns 7
     @My::dependency( 0 ); // returns 6
     @My::dependency( 8 ); // returns 7
 };
 // verify
 Moxie.Verify();
 // teardown
 Destroy( Moxie );
This is a type safe mock; it will not allow you to match using incorrect parameter/return types.
This mock is strict, ie each call must be specified in advance, and in order.
For Typed Structure instances, Moxie::Typed Structure provides an equivalent API (see below).
Using argument matchers with Moxie
You can also match each expected argument value using a predicate:


 Link( "_LIB Value Match" ); // for matchers like @Is::Greater Than()
 Moxie = Moxie::Function( Target:= "My::Dependency" )
     .Call( \x-> Mod( x, 2 ) )            . Returning( 9 )
     .Call( That( Is::Greater Than, 5 ) ) . Returning( 10 )
 ;
For more information see Example: Moxie. For matchers that may be useful, see _LIB Value Match.

RegTest::Smock
RegTest::Smock uses the mockito replay/verify style:


 Link( "_Slang RegTest Stub Function" );
 Link( "_TYPE RegTest Smock" );
 // specify 
 Smock = RegTest::Smock( );
 Mock = Smock.Mock( "My::Dependency" );
 Mock.Then Return( 6 );                               
 Mock.Then Return( 7, When Args := [ 2 ] );           
 // replay
 RegTestStubFunction( Smock.Overrides() )
 {
     @My::dependency( 2 ); // returns 7                   
     @My::dependency( 0 ); // returns 6                   
     @My::dependency( 8 ); // returns Null!
 };
 // verify
 Assert( Mock.Verify( Args := [ 0 ], Times := 1 ) );
 Assert( Mock.Verify( Args := [ 2 ] ) );
 // teardown
 Destroy( Smock );
Caveats
RegTestStubFunction is not inherently typesafe with respect to function signature, so if the dependency changes, your test may pass regardless. Use Moxie::Function with a target to get a typesafe mock.
To mitigate the risk of being too isolated from your dependency, write additional tests that don't use isolation.
Don't use so many mocks that your test is sensitive to every implementation detail. (overspecifying). As an extreme example, never mock pure functions such as Array::Union.
Common problems
"You cannot stub out unlinked variable 'Scope::Fn Name'"
The Probable Fix
Make your function reference more explicit by adding the library script name. The library will be linked automatically. So:


   RegTestStubFunction( Structure(
           "_LIB Some Lib::Scope::Fn Name", Value,
   ) )
   { ... }
(See FAQ for similar issues accessing shared Private scope.)

A Less Probable, But Possible, Fix
You can also get the "You cannot stub out unlinked variable 'Scope::Fn Name'" exception when there is a compilation error in the script defining the function you're stubbing, or in a script that the defining script depends on. For example, see Review 20240725 6010-0783396S*.

Value func 'RegTestStubFunction' does not bind a block ( missing ';' ? )
If your script is failing to compile with something like this,

SlangParser: Script has an error at (or near) '{'
 SlangParser: Line#   23 Column#    0
 SlangParser: Value func 'RegTestStubFunction' does not bind a block (missing ';' ?)
you forgot the
   Link( "_Slang RegTest Stub Function" );
I want to mock multiple functions at the same time!
This is easy, create multiple mocks and then register them all before the block with the expected calls, for example:


 Link( "_TYPE Moxie" );
 // record
 Moxie 1 = Moxie::Function( Target := "My::First Dependency" )
     .Call( 0 ).Returning( 6 );
 Moxie 2 = Moxie::Function( Target := "My::Second Dependency" )
     .Call( 1 ).Returning( 7 );

 RegTestStubFunction( Moxie 1.Overrides() ++ Moxie 2.Overrides() )
 {
     @My::First Dependency( 0 );
     @My::Second Dependency( 1 );
 };
 // verify
 Moxie 1.Verify();
 Moxie 2.Verify();
My mocks stop working after editing my test
If you are finding the following: mocks work for the first time you run them but after editing your test they don't work in subsequent runs and do work if you start a new session, try using the backout: BACKOUT_SET_EXPORTS_IN_USERDBS_6010_3066452 (so on windows use 'set BACKOUT_SET_EXPORTS_IN_USERDBS_6010_3066452=Quiet' in the cmd, and on linux use 'export BACKOUT_SET_EXPORTS_IN_USERDBS_6010_3066452=Quiet').

